# Findings Trivy should stop reporting, and why.
#
# Read by both scans: the filesystem scan in ci.yml (dependencies, secrets, Dockerfile
# misconfiguration) and the image scan in deploy.yml, which runs between `docker build`
# and `docker push` so a CRITICAL never reaches Artifact Registry.
#
# Before adding a line here, check that the answer is not simply to upgrade. Both scans
# already pass `--ignore-unfixed`, so anything reaching this file HAS a released fix —
# which makes an entry here a decision to ship a known-vulnerable dependency. That can be
# the right call (the vulnerable code path is unreachable from this app, the fix needs a
# major upgrade that has to be planned), but it is a decision, and it needs a name and a
# date so the next person can tell a considered exception from an abandoned one.
#
# Format — one CVE per line, with the two comment lines above it:
#
#   # <why this is acceptable here — the specific reason, not "false positive">
#   # <who / YYYY-MM-DD / when to look again>
#   CVE-2026-12345
#
# Review this file whenever the deploy checklist is revisited. An exception with a
# review date in the past is a finding of its own.
#
# What these scans do NOT prove: the secret scanner matches well-known token formats
# (GitHub, AWS, Stripe and similar). It does not recognise every provider's key shape —
# a Clerk secret key or a database URL with a password in it passes straight through. It
# is a net for the obvious mistake, not a guarantee that nothing sensitive is committed.
#
# Nothing is ignored at the moment: the current tree scans clean at HIGH and CRITICAL.
